TV is better than it's ever been and  with that explosion in choice and  quality comes a quantity of incredible  performances here are the actors whose  performances go beyond great and are  perfectly flawless the three short years  from 2013 and 2015 were a magical era in  entertainment rising from the ashes of  crappy romantic comedies and punchlines  about how often he could be spotted  shirtless Matthew McConaughey uttered in  the Makana sense it was a career  reinvention in which he proved himself a  compelling leading man who could  disappear into any role without even  ditching his thick Texas accent while  the film Dallas buyers club may have  been the one that scored him an Oscar  and interstellar marked the most  high-profile film of that era there's no  role he took during that era that has  the cultural impact of rust coal from  the HBO series true detective Cole is a  bleak nihilistic detective worn down  from years of undercover work loss and  substance abuse he never gives a  straight answer to any question managing  to turn something as simple as how do  you sleep into a riddle  he's simultaneously obnoxious and  endearing despite sounding like a 15  year old who's just seen Fight Club for  the first time your master laugh  maybe McConaughey excels in the role  playing off Woody Harrelson who co-stars  as Cole's partner Marty with Odd Couple  chemistry Cole could read as parody if  not for the nuanced McConaughey brings  to the role any future true detective  actors have big shoes to fill  anyone who has a complicated  relationship with a family member is  going to find Netflix's bloodline way  too real to get through it in a single  sitting the show analyzes the complex  family dynamics between parents siblings  and extended family in an  extraordinarily raw fashion there's no  poor acting in the series and Kyle  Chandler is truly exceptional as John  the patriarch of the Rayburn family that  said nobody knocks it out of the park  quite like ben mendelsohn as Danny the  black sheep of the family  Danny's arrival in the show's pilot  signals a proverbial storm on the  horizon he's effectively been  excommunicated from the Rayburn clan and  it quickly becomes clear why he's wild  and not in the sense of a constantly  partying frat boy he's more like a live  wire dangling over running water it  doesn't take much to set him off and  when that happens something's going to  be burned to the ground  Mendelssohn's portrayal is darkly  charismatic and very careful in its  steps a necessity considering Danny's  tendency to pivot sharply from friendly  to terrifying in a matter of seconds  those turns never seem unbelievable  thanks to the care Mendelssohn puts into  the role his avatar of chaos is a true  standout not just for this show but for  the entirety of Netflix's original  content bloodlines is very good overall  but Mendelssohn's performance is what  makes it can't miss binge television  show times penny dreadful probably could  have had a decade-long run if it merely  stuck to its roots in gothic horror and  fantasy playing up the poppier elements  of its source material the show revolved  around a fictional universe in which  Victorian horror icons such as Dorian  Gray and Victor Frankenstein exist in  the same world a winning formula for a  19th century version of supernatural  instead the show chose to become  thing more it cast a crew of exceptional  British actors and played up the  Shakespearean overtones of its story  elevating the artistic integrity of its  genre roots to the forefront while the  whole cast is exceptional  former James Bond actor Timothy Dalton  is especially good as an ex adventurer  Malcolm Murray the standout is easily  ava Green as Vanessa Vanessa is a woman  whose entire life has been surrounded by  darkness by otherworldly forces seeking  to use her to their whims but her  strength lies in her constant refusal to  be anything but a bright light in her  dark world you could argue for Green's  excellence in the role based solely on  the extent to which she seems to  disregard her own physical well-being  for the sake of performance  Vanessa goes through several drastic  physical transformations and a scene  involving a seance sees her contorting  her body gruesomely however to focus on  her physical commitment would be to sell  short the empathy and tenderness Green  instills into Vanessa very few  characters in this mold have felt so  undeniably human penny dreadful is all  the better for it there aren't a lot of  comedies that have balanced a gradual  transition from conventional to bizarre  quite like new girl over the course of  its run it transformed from a quirky  Zooey Deschanel vehicle to a truly  out-there comedy about some of the  strangest characters on TV few  characters embody that evolution quite  like Nick Miller play to perfection by  Jake Johnson you ought to be a long old  truck or the family how the family would  be in Montana or or Mars landing you  know if the government finally steps up  Nick enters the series as a comedic  straight man pretty conventional and  clearly set up to be the Ross to  Deschanel's Rachel his neuroses and  quirks are dealt out slowly over the  first two seasons and by the time season  4 begins he's a full-on nutcase and a  pretty endearing one at that he's an  emotionally stunted conspiracy theorist  whose best friend is a silent man he  talks to on a park bench Johnson  embodies Nick perfectly especially  shining when doing or saying something  ridiculous that Nick clearly believes is  entirely normal he brings a great deal  of heart to the role that keeps Nick  and it even when he's going off about  his distrust of banks and cell phones  taking a character from a normal place  to such a weird one is tricky to pull  off for a writers room luckily they had  Johnson to anchor the character and make  it all work in the wake of get out and  black panther it's easy to forget we're  now superstar actor Daniel Kuya got one  of his first major exposures to American  audiences an early episode of the  pessimistic sci-fi anthology black  mirror  Kaluga appears in the series second ever  episode titled 50 million merits and  it's a performance that shows exactly  why he rocketed to worldwide fame  shortly after this was something real  time  just once each episode of the show  explores the disquieting implications of  an aspect of technology or media in this  case the story revolves around a sort of  twisted reality show in a dystopian and  close-quarters world in which the only  way one can rise from their societal  rank as a grunt working an exercise bike  all day is to win an American Idol ask  gameshow we explore this world through a  character named Bing and follow his  romance with a fellow biker only to see  her sucked into the world of this  reality show it's an almost  Shakespearean tragedy and being played  by Cal Ouya is among black mirrors most  memorable characters we follow him from  frustration to elation elation to love  love to heartbreak and heartbreak to an  unending seething rage it's a difficult  character arc to tackle especially in a  single episode of high concept  television but coleus chops are clearly  up to the task when it comes to the  all-time greats of superhero casting  sure Chris Evans and Robert Downey jr.  are great but they've got small screen  competition Krysten Ritter as Jessica  Jones in the Netflix show of the same  name definitely belongs on the list of  all-time great superhero casting it's  all the more impressive when you realize  that Jones is unlike any other  protagonist viewers have seen in the  genre and Ritter delivers an incredible  TV performance to do it proper justice  Jessica Jones has superpowers but she's  no Avenger I gave it a shot once tell me  there was a costume when you still got  it  it didn't work out instead she's a  private investigator a combination of  every detective novel stereotype with  the addition of superhuman strength and  a few other cool powers when we meet her  we see a different kind of Marvel hero a  victim of abuse at the hands of a  sadistic villain who used mind control  to bend her to his winds Britta is  dealing with emotions and traumas that  are incredibly real ones that viewers of  the show are far more likely to have  experienced themselves than those of  Thor or Captain America as such Britta  pairs a great deal of responsibility in  performing this role and she handles it  with poise and grace masterfully  navigating Jessica through a multitude  of complex emotional highs and lows it  also definitely doesn't hurt that she's  great at sardonically quipping her way  through every confrontation with a bad  guy that comes her way if true detective  is HBO's effort to showcase the skill of  two massively talented male actors big  little lies is their effort to do the  same for a group of some of the most  talented female actors on the planet  every woman on the show is tremendous  from Laura Dern to Nicole Kidman  but Reese Witherspoon's performance is  perhaps the most memorable as Madeline  Witherspoon is a seemingly simple  character with a wealth of complications  beneath her surface she's a woman with  an all too real desire to convince her  neighbors and family that everything is  fine Madeline is on her second marriage  and struggling with raising an older  daughter who's in the dregs of her  teenage years and a younger daughter  who's already too perceptive for her own  good she constantly runs into her ex who  parades around town with his new younger  wife clearly looking to rub it in  Madeline space oh I'm loved there oh  thank you I made it in crew course you  did Witherspoon channels the defensive  combative bluster into Madeline and  abrasiveness that keeps people at two  greater distance to see how close she is  to crumbling it's a remarkable  performance in a show already chock full  of them when Hollywood came calling with  roles in blockbusters like Pacific Rim  and Marvel's Thor films Idris Elba  easily could have stepped away from his  role as police detective John Luther in  the BBC series Luther  hasn't the show has amidst Elvis ascent  as a movie draw continued on a clear  testament to how much he enjoys playing  the character it makes perfect sense as  the Luthor is by far the most compelling  role he's ever taken on and a forum for  some of the most consistently great work  of his career John Luther is a bull in a  china shop he's a ruthless violent  detective with intense anger issues and  a disregard for any authority that isn't  his he'd probably be a villain if he  weren't so dang cool that might be  what's so impressive about Elvis  performances Luther in addition to  tackling such a wide array of dark  emotions he never forgets to make Luther  the coolest cop on TV from his icy  demeanor to his trademark tweed jacket  you can't help but want to be him save  his anger management issues in constant  barrage of tragedy that is it's been a  long time since the era of bonanza and  Gunsmoke had westerns ruling the TV  airwaves but make no mistake American  audiences still love a good cowboy the  fewer and further between these days but  there's nothing like a TV show about a  straight-up old-school gunslinger no  show has updated that classic archetype  to the modern era quite as well as  justified which features Timothy  Olyphant as Raylan Givens a u.s. marshal  whose cowboy ways don't stop at his hat  and sub and rule why don't you  understand I don't pull my sidearm less  I'm going to shoot to kill that's his  purpose huh to kill so how I use it the  show follows Raylan originally created  by legendary crime novelist Elmore  Leonard as he's forced to relocate to  his hometown of Harlan Kentucky after a  shootout gone wrong in Miami aside from  taking on criminals of all sorts  Raylan is confronted with old friends  enemies and family ties he's made every  effort to escape since leaving  underneath the cowboy cool demeanor and  quick-draw Oliphant instills a simmering  anger in Raylan one that's amplified by  how well Oliphant performs the  characters quieter moments we love a  classic western shootout but Raylan is  never more engaging than he is when  talking to his ex-wife his antagonist  Boyd played immaculately by Walton  Goggins or confronting his father  elephant brings equal charisma and  empathy to a character that  could be a one-note gunslinger instead  he delivers one of the most memorable TV  performances and easily the greatest  cowboy character of televisions modern  era check out one of our newest videos  right here plus even more looper videos  about your favorite shows are coming  soon subscribe to our YouTube channel  and hit the bowl so you don't miss a  single one  [Music]
 

